## Summary

Concrete migration of the `developer-products` Resource under
[ADR-022](docs/adr/022-by-domain-folder-layout-for-ocale.md) (by-Domain
folder layout for `@bedrock/ocale`).

- Move the wire code for the three Operations on `/developer-products/v2/...`
  (`get`, `create`, `update`) from the peer Resource folder
  `src/resources/developer-products/` into a per-shape sub-tree at
  `src/domains/developer-products/products/`. The Resource folder collapses
  to its public surface (`client.ts`, `index.ts`, `index.spec-d.ts`);
  builders, parsers, operation-limit constants, wire types, and parsed
  shapes now live in the Domain sub-tree.
- The local `toBlob` helper in `builders.ts` is dropped in favour of the
  shared `internal/utils/to-blob.ts` already used by the existing
  `game-internationalization` Domain (introduced by #300).
- Conformance tests (`tests/conformance/developer-products.spec.ts`),
  integration tests (`tests/integration/resources/developer-products/client.spec.ts`),
  and the wire-body helper (`tests/helpers/developer-products.ts`) are
  re-pointed at the new locations without behavioural change.

Wire requests, parsed shapes, and rate-limit operation keys
(`developer-products.{get,create,update}`) are preserved end-to-end.

## Why

ADR-022 mandates the by-Domain layout for `@bedrock/ocale` going forward,
and #300 proved the convention against `game-internationalization`. Issue
\#302 is the second concrete migration: it brings `developer-products` in
line so the remaining peer-folder Resources (`places/`, `game-passes/`,
`universes/`) can follow the same pattern in subsequent issues.

Closes #302.

